1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,856
Aziz uses three apps from his bank: the mobile bank, the marketplace and the credit service.

2
00:00:05,906 --> 00:00:08,304
Each app must know that it is really Aziz.

3
00:00:08,354 --> 00:00:11,760
But nobody wants a face check every time they open an app.

4
00:00:11,810 --> 00:00:20,432
So how can a bank know it is really Aziz, in every app, without checking his face every time?

5
00:00:20,482 --> 00:00:27,128
This course builds the answer: heeyID, a customer identity platform that the bank runs inside its own walls.

6
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:30,848
Follow Aziz for one day, and the whole map appears.

7
00:00:30,898 --> 00:00:33,104
Aziz installs the mobile bank.

8
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:39,128
It is the master app: it moves money, so only it may run MyID and TAF.

9
00:00:39,178 --> 00:00:43,064
He types his phone number, and an SMS code arrives.

10
00:00:43,114 --> 00:00:48,416
It comes through TAF, the state antifraud service of the mobile operators.

11
00:00:48,466 --> 00:00:54,008
TAF checks that the code arrived on this phone, with this SIM card, and its verdict is green.

12
00:00:54,058 --> 00:00:58,016
He types the code, then sets a password and types it a second time.

13
00:00:58,066 --> 00:01:01,328
This one password opens every app of the bank.

14
00:01:01,378 --> 00:01:03,848
Now Aziz has an account at level one.

15
00:01:03,898 --> 00:01:07,160
The bank knows his phone, but it does not yet know the person.

16
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:10,472
In the marketplace, Aziz wants to buy a phone on credit.

17
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:15,824
The credit service needs level two: it must know the real person, with a real passport.

18
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:21,680
The marketplace is a side app, and it never checks a face, so it sends Aziz to the mobile bank.

19
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:25,568
There, the app first asks for his consent, and records it.

20
00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:31,112
Then MyID, the national identity service, opens inside the mobile bank.

21
00:01:31,162 --> 00:01:34,784
It checks his face and his passport against the state records.

22
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:38,864
The platform also checks his phone number against the numbers that MyID knows.

23
00:01:38,914 --> 00:01:41,264
Aziz is now at level two.

24
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:47,792
Next, the platform looks for Aziz in the bank's own customer records, by his personal identification number.

25
00:01:47,842 --> 00:01:49,880
It finds him, and links the two.

26
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,184
His phone becomes a trusted device.

27
00:01:52,234 --> 00:01:59,192
Back in the marketplace, Aziz taps Approve in the mobile bank, and the marketplace session gets level two too.

28
00:01:59,242 --> 00:02:05,000
Each app gets its own access grant: this is Aziz, at level two, for this app only.

29
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:09,392
Next morning, Aziz opens the marketplace, then the mobile bank.

30
00:02:09,442 --> 00:02:11,264
No face check.

31
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:13,136
No new code.

32
00:02:13,186 --> 00:02:16,232
His sessions simply continue on his trusted phone.

33
00:02:16,282 --> 00:02:19,208
One proofing serves every app of the bank.

34
00:02:19,258 --> 00:02:21,080
Now the worry.

35
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:25,976
A stranger talks the mobile operator into moving Aziz's number to a new SIM card.

36
00:02:26,026 --> 00:02:31,184
He puts it in a new phone, installs the mobile bank and tries to log in as Aziz.

37
00:02:31,234 --> 00:02:33,056
He even guessed the password.

38
00:02:33,106 --> 00:02:36,928
What stops him?

39
00:02:36,978 --> 00:02:42,592
Not TAF alone: the SIM card really has Aziz's number, so the verdict can be green.

40
00:02:42,642 --> 00:02:48,976
But it is a new phone, and Aziz was at level two, so the mobile bank asks for MyID on this phone.

41
00:02:49,026 --> 00:02:52,072
The stranger's face is not Aziz's face.

42
00:02:52,122 --> 00:02:59,776
At the same time, Aziz's trusted phone shows the login request, with two buttons: Approve, and This was not me.

43
00:02:59,826 --> 00:03:03,232
Aziz presses This was not me, and the login stops.

44
00:03:03,282 --> 00:03:08,872
His number now goes on hold: no codes and no SMS to it, only push.

45
00:03:08,922 --> 00:03:15,184
So the marketplace web cannot help the stranger either: its login request also goes to Aziz's phone.

46
00:03:15,234 --> 00:03:17,632
Aziz changes his password at once.

47
00:03:17,682 --> 00:03:20,680
Then the app asks him: does your SIM card still work?

48
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:27,592
It does not, so Aziz passes MyID again, the number leaves his account, and Aziz adds a new one.

49
00:03:27,642 --> 00:03:29,512
Now look at the map behind that day.

50
00:03:29,562 --> 00:03:34,864
The bank builds its own apps, and every app calls the heeyID API.

51
00:03:34,914 --> 00:03:40,144
Only the mobile bank holds the MyID and TAF SDKs.

52
00:03:40,194 --> 00:03:46,648
The other apps are side apps: they use the password, then an approval in the mobile bank or an SMS code.

53
00:03:46,698 --> 00:03:51,928
Every request from the apps enters through one edge, and goes to the customer gateway.

54
00:03:51,978 --> 00:03:59,032
All the rules of the bank live in that gateway: levels, limits, devices, sessions and consent.

55
00:03:59,082 --> 00:04:01,336
Behind the gateway is Keycloak.

56
00:04:01,386 --> 00:04:05,200
It does one job: it issues the tokens for the access grant.

57
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:12,520
Each app is one client, and each token names its app, so a marketplace token never opens the mobile bank.

58
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:15,856
The gateway never calls the outside world directly.

59
00:04:15,906 --> 00:04:24,472
It uses adapters: one each for TAF, MyID, the SMS service, the customer records and the push service.

60
00:04:24,522 --> 00:04:28,024
In the mobile bank, TAF sends the codes.

61
00:04:28,074 --> 00:04:34,528
The bank's SMS service sends them in the side apps, and in the mobile bank only when TAF is down.

62
00:04:34,578 --> 00:04:37,072
Behind the gateway are four stores.

63
00:04:37,122 --> 00:04:38,944
Postgres keeps the records.

64
00:04:38,994 --> 00:04:42,184
Valkey keeps the counters and the cool-downs.

65
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:45,592
Kafka carries every event out, through an outbox.

66
00:04:45,642 --> 00:04:47,464
Vault keeps the keys.

67
00:04:47,514 --> 00:04:50,296
The bank's staff work in the administrator panel.

68
00:04:50,346 --> 00:04:55,264
It talks to the admin gateway, which uses the same rules, not a second copy.

69
00:04:55,314 --> 00:04:59,560
Now draw one frame around all of it: the bank's perimeter.

70
00:04:59,610 --> 00:05:04,432
One installation serves one bank, and a second bank gets its own installation.

71
00:05:04,482 --> 00:05:09,064
TAF reaches the platform through its own agent in the bank's network.

72
00:05:09,114 --> 00:05:14,296
Only MyID and the push service are outside, and the push service gets no personal data.

73
00:05:14,346 --> 00:05:21,016
To recap: Aziz proved who he is one time, and his trusted phone carries that proof to every app of the bank.

74
00:05:21,066 --> 00:05:25,536
So how many times does Aziz meet MyID?

75
00:05:25,586 --> 00:05:29,640
Once, and again only on a new phone, or when something risky happens.

76
00:05:29,690 --> 00:05:34,416
Next: the words of identity, from authentication to assurance level.
